  "query_preview": "..\n\nA vertically stacked mid-upper level low, centered over southern\nwill slowly track east into Wyoming through tonight while \ngradually weakening. As the system continues eastward tonight, a \nfew lingering showers remain possible across southwestern Montana \ninto Sunday morning as moisture wraps around its backside. \nClearing skies from the west overnight into Sunday morning may \nfavor fog formation in southwestern Montana valleys. Upper-level \nridging will then rebuild across Montana and the Northern Rockies \nby Sunday afternoon, returning predominantly sunny, warmer, and \ndrier conditions to the area.\n\nA dry weather pattern with above-normal temperatures is expected \nnext week. Broad-scale ridging will attempt to build westward across \nthe CONUS while troughing persists off the West Coast, maintaining \nupper-level heights above average under a southwesterly flow regime. \nHigh temperatures in the upper 70s to around 80 are anticipated, \nrunning roughly 10 degrees above early-autumn normals. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHi-Res Ensemble Forecast (HREF) guidance supports a 20-30% chance\nfor reduced visibilities across Southwest and Central Montana\nwhere recent precipitation has fallen, which seems reasonable\nconsidering uncertainty with how fast skies clear tonight. \nConfidence is higher in fog development for areas west of the US \nHwy 287 corridor where skies will clear well before sunrise on \nSunday, with decreasing confidence east of this line. Where fog is\nable to develop there is the potential for locally dense fog \ngiven antecedent low level moisture and light winds. - Moldan",
